<h2>Guidance for the reader</h2>
<h3>What this is</h3>
<p>An approved state. Somebody named in the header accepted the capture this baseline was made from, gave a reason and a change id, and signed the result.</p>
<h3>What it answers</h3>
<p>What was accepted, by whom, when, and for which capture. The capture_digest below ties this approval to exactly one capture; no other capture can be substituted for it.</p>
<h3>What it does not answer</h3>
<p>Whether the signature is valid, and whether the approver was entitled to approve. This page shows the signature block as the bundle records it. Validity is a question for the verifier, with a key you trust, and this page is not that verifier. Whether the approver was entitled is a question for your process.</p>
<h3>What to do next</h3>
<p>Keep it, and compare later captures of the same host against it:</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze diff --baseline &lt;baseline&gt; --current &lt;capture&gt; --trusted-key &lt;public key&gt;</pre>
<h3>To check the bundle behind this page (integrity, signature and trust, offline):</h3>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze verify --bundle &lt;bundle&gt; --trusted-key &lt;public key&gt;</pre>
<p class="note">The key must be one you obtained out of band. A signature verifies against the key you hand it; it cannot tell you that the key belongs to the right person.</p>

