<h3>The probes</h3>
<p class="note">The status is the word the bundle carries, written out. Only captured means "collected in full".
partial, unsupported, unavailable, permission_denied, timeout, failed and not_applicable each mean something
else, and the reason beside the word is what the probe itself reported. A host whose report says
permission_denied three times is not clean there; it is unchecked there.</p>
<table>
<caption>Every probe of this capture</caption>
<tr><th class="name">Probe</th><th>Status</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td class="name mono">common.identity</td><td class="mono">captured</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
<tr><td class="name mono">linux.packages</td><td class="mono">unsupported</td><td>not_implemented</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Who may do what on this host</h3>
<p>This bundle carries no capabilities document, so nobody resolved capabilities for this capture. That is
not the same as a host that grants nothing: the question was not asked here. The command below shows which
documents the bundle does carry.</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze verify --bundle &lt;bundle&gt;</pre>


<h2>Not on this page</h2>
<p class="note">What this page leaves out is named here, with the command that shows it. Nothing is left out
silently.</p>
<h3>The artifacts of this capture: 2 entries.</h3>
<p>One row per artifact is a stack of paper and not a printout, and the list carries the host name, every local account, every listening address and the fingerprint of every authorized key. The count is on this page so that nothing is hidden, and the report document has every entry.</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze report --input &lt;bundle&gt; --output &lt;file.json&gt;</pre>
<h3>The full result of each probe: 2 documents, with every command a probe ran and the state it normalized.</h3>
<p>The table above names every probe with its status and its reason in full; the raw results repeat the artifacts and add the invocation records.</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze report --input &lt;bundle&gt; --output &lt;file.json&gt;</pre>

